        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pseudo-uroliths are primarily a disease of cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dried solidified blood pseudo-uroliths comprise 1% or less of feline uroliths. They are 2–3 times more common in male than female cats. The mean age of affected cats is 9 years old (range 1–15 years).",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ectopic bone pseudo-uroliths are rare, with few cases reported.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary tract obstructions (ureteral or urethral) are common. Obstructions can result in uremia with anorexia, vomiting, weakness, and, if hyperkalemia is present, bradycardia. Urethral obstructions can also cause nonproductive stranguria and a palpably enlarged, firm bladder.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Other signs include hematuria, dysuria, and pollakiuria.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dried solidified blood pseudo-uroliths are associated with hematuria, but mechanisms underlying their formation are unknown. Concurrent urinary tract infections are occasionally present.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ectopic bone forms due to ossification of the urothelium. Trauma and inflammation are risk factors in humans, but their role in veterinary species has not been determined.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Consider more common lower urinary tract diseases, such as feline idiopathic lower urinary tract disease, other types of urolithiasis (e.g., calcium oxalate and struvite), urethral plugs, urinary tract infection, and urinary tract neoplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hematuria, proteinuria, and, if obstruction is present, azotemia and hyperkalemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1460,
              "printed_page": 1378,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
